title: IainSchweblinClue0
---
In the middle of a cold winter night, a prepubescent Iain awoke to a strange sound coming from his closet. This cacophony of clicking slowly began to push him out of a deep sleep. Resenting the interruption, he rose and walked toward the sound, yanking open the closet door. The moonlight from a nearby window illuminated the undulating wall of termites swarming behind that door. Their translucent bodies and sharp pincers scurried erratically up and down the walls, feasting on the wood as they left behind rows of tiny holes in a meticulous, unsettling pattern Iain screamed and jumped back, shaking and batting at his arms, certain they would pierce him, too, over and over, until he was as annihilated as the wall.
===
title: IainSchweblinClue1
---
Iain wasn’t one of the cool kids at Morrison Middle School. He didn’t talk much and, when he did, his words came out in an awkward jumble. A couple of older, paunchy kids made a hobby out of picking on him. One day as he sat down alone in the cafeteria, Iain opened his lunch box to find three large beetles burrowing into his sandwich. He yelled an incoherent curse and threw it to the floor. The two boys laughed as he ran from the room, their broad bellies jiggling as they mocked his terrified reaction.
===
title: IainSchweblinClue2
---
Iain knew he wasn’t the only kid who didn’t want to go to the doctor. During the twenty minute drive to get there, he dreaded the harsh bright lights. The antiseptic smell. The creepy old nurses who shushed his protests. When a routine visit required lab tests, Iain sat in his mother’s lap as a young assistant came in to draw his blood. He squeezed his eyes shut, silently counting to himself as he waited for the jab of pain, the seconds of suction and then the words that would confirm it was over. “All done.” But when he opened his eyes, he saw a dark red spurt shoot straight up, splattering crimson drops on the white lab coat. Shocked by the velocity and volume of blood, she pressed her palm against the puncture as she struggled to reach the nearby bandages. He sees that moment replay in his mind every time he has to go to the doctor, remembering the smear of his own blood on the frazzled woman’s cheek as she turned to leave him.
===